00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.630 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  2022 could be a historic year for china  xi jinping is preparing to ask the  communist party congress for a third  term in office  what will this mean for the rest of the  world  and is conflict with america inevitable  [Music]  there's a lot to unpack around china and  it can be hard to really understand  what's going on so ed what does 2021  taught you about this man she and ping's  vision for china and indeed the  communist party's vision for china and  if they already the same the same thing  i see this year as uh xi jinping  preparing for the really major event  which is going to come sometime next  autumn  with a party congress when he breaks the  mould and becomes the first leader since  mao to have a third  term  and that and that could lead to a fourth  term and in order to do this he not only  has to exert his control over the  country but also set out an agenda a  reason a justification in fact for why  he needs to have this third five-year  term  and this is i think absolutely  fascinating because his agenda is a mix  of things that worry us  uh in in the west things like the  dominance of consumer tech the monopoly  power of the big tech companies the way  they use data  um inequality the sense that uh perhaps  you know our children spend too long  playing computer games and it's not very  good for them so a lot of a lot of these  concerns actually are shared between the  western china but he deals with them in  an incredibly authoritarian way and a  way that absolutely centralizes party  power so for instance clamping down on  the consumer tech companies destroying  perhaps we reckoned in october about  some like 2 trillion worth of value  you see the aggressive attitude towards  anyone who rocks the boat peng  schweither the tennis player and the  treatment of her so this is an  authoritarian way of dealing with a lot  of problems that the whole world faces  and i think it's in that kind of mix  it's a hugely complex and interesting  constellation of forces of  authoritarianism and taking on the big  problems of society  and i mean we will think and talk a lot  about what that means for the rest of  the world but what in practice does that  mean for people who live within china's  borders  well i think we need to remember  that  xi jinping and the communist party are  popular in china chinese nationalism and  pride within china is at an all-time  high  the chinese people feel that they're  doing very well in the world they look  at kovid for example and they see you  know the millions of deaths around the  world including in advanced democracies  and they think that their system strange  as it seems to also perhaps locking  everyone down as soon as a case arises  they see their extraordinary low death  rate as a mark of tremendous success  now of course uh there are people who  are unhappy about this china has a a bad  record for arresting journalists over a  million wiggers have been through these  re-education accounts in xinjiang so  this this is not nice for everybody by  any means but i don't i think we'd be  kidding ourselves in the west if we  thought that um the chinese people see  xi jinping and the ccp through the same  eyes as we do zany um did 2021 sort of  mark the the death of big private uh  enterprise in china what's going on what  do you make of that landscape  no i don't think it marked the death of  big private enterprise but it definitely  marked the very  serious clampdown and assertion of party  control and private business serves at  the sort of uh behest of and in further  to further the goals of the party  because it's just  extraordinary the scale of the domestic  shift in this year in this year before  the national park the communist party  congress that uh ed talked about the  assertion of sea's power the clampdown  on business as you say but also the  shift internationally and china is  determined as xi jinping says to you  know move to center stage globally it's  becoming more assertive internationally  even as it's becoming more authoritarian  and indeed autocratic at home there were  hopes that with you know the move from  trump to buy them there might be a  thawing of relations but that that  doesn't really seem to be um the case at  all a lot of people quickly jump to the  conclusion that this is a new cold war  is is the current u.s china relationship  indeed beginning a sort of new cold war  was that actually the wrong  lens through which to um look at  what might be happening with this sort  of neo-forming world order the original  cold war  had between the soviet union and the and  the west um was an ideological struggle  between two sort of parts of the world  that had remarkably little economically  to do with each other there was very  little trade um meaningful trade between  the two blocks what we have between the  us and china is something fundamentally  different this is the world's first  biggest and second biggest economy  incredibly interlinked modern  globalization is sort of built on those  linkages and there are lots and lots of  countries including the fastest growing  countries in asia that are hugely  integrated with the chinese economy  while also relying on the american  security guarantee and if you look at  the implications of that for biden  they're rather awkward because the more  he tries to galvanize support at home by  by kind of you know banging the drama  and calling this new cold war the hardy  he makes his job of building alliances  in southeast asia there are plenty of  countries there that look to the united  states for their security but look to  china for their prosperity and you if  you try and force them to choose america  might just find that they make the  choice for china rather than for the  united states taiwan comes up a lot um  as a question right so so what will  ultimately happen the status quo here is  full of tension and ambiguity and a  series of sort of threats that balance  each other out  and people are always jostling for  position between the taiwanese  government flirting with independence  but not too much try to show it does not  want to  join up with china as it at the moment  with xi jinping and the chinese  government for whom it is an absolute  national priority  to acquire taiwan and if we're thinking  over 20 or 30 years i think sort of they  will try and see that to a close but  over the next decade decade perhaps not  and then the united states where what's  staked there is it's its entire  strategic presence in the  pacific because the united states  does nothing to protect taiwan then all  its pr its promises to south korea and  japan are hugely undermined  so this is a this is the naughtiest of  of naughty problems and when i say  status quo i don't mean to suggest it's  going to be easy but i think the risks  for everybody including xi jinping of  moving right now are just too great  um so  i think it can be managed but but i  don't think it's comfortable for anybody  understanding what is going on in china  understanding what's happening where  it's going is is the single most  important job frankly that we have to do  and that you know it's nothing  nothing else is as dramatic i think in  its consequences for every other big  issue in the world so it's it's  enormously fascinating it's quite scary  um in many ways very difficult to make  sense of not least because it is you  know very very hard to get a sense of  what's going on in china but nothing  could be more important  hi this was part of a digital event  produced exclusively for subscribers to  the economist if you'd like to watch the  full discussion of this or of one of our  other events then click the link you'll  just have to register if you haven't  already or you can become an economist  subscriber to enjoy access to all of our  journalism thanks for watching

